# SiyahTuş pilot — the reverse proxy in front of docker-compose.pilot.yml (tm 256.5).
#
# LATER, AND DNS-ONLY (tm 257.10). The pilot's edge today is a Cloudflare
# Tunnel (infra/pilot/cloudflared/config.example.yml), serving the same four
# names from the same ports. This file is for when the host is reached
# directly: the four DNS records must be DNS-only (grey cloud). Behind an
# orange (proxied) record it is NOT supported. Caddy would replace
# X-Forwarded-For with the Cloudflare edge's address, so every visitor would
# collapse onto the edge. Fixing that needs `trusted_proxies` here plus
# TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=2, and apps/api/src/config/env.pilot.test.ts forbids
# `trusted_proxies` ("never believes an X-Forwarded-For the client sent").
# Switching edges is stopping cloudflared and starting Caddy; compose and .env
# do not change.
#
# The compose file publishes its four ports on 127.0.0.1 only; this is the one
# public side. Caddy runs on the same host (not in the compose project — it
# dials 127.0.0.1), obtains and renews the TLS certificates itself, and serves
# four hostnames. Replace `example.com` with the pilot's domain; each name then
# goes into `.env`:
#
#   panel.example.com   WEB_APP_URL, and the first origin in WEB_ORIGIN
#   widget.example.com  WIDGET_BASE_URL, and the second origin in WEB_ORIGIN
#   api.example.com     API_BASE_URL  (https://)
#   rtm.example.com     RTM_BASE_URL  (wss://)
#
# The ports below are the compose defaults. A `SIYAHTUS_*_HOST_PORT` line in
# `.env` moves one; change the matching line here with it.
#
# Why every path is one hop, and why that matters: the api derives the caller's
# address (rate limit, IP ban, agent IP allow-list) from X-Forwarded-For and
# trusts exactly TRUST_PROXY_HOPS entries of it — one number for every path.
# The panel's nginx also proxies `/api/`, and it appends to X-Forwarded-For, so
# a panel request sent through it would arrive with two hops while the widget's
# arrives with one. The panel site therefore sends `/api/*` straight to the
# api, past the panel's nginx, and TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1 is right for all four
# names (docs/production-checklist.md §9, "Reverse proxy").
#
# Caddy does not believe an X-Forwarded-For the client sent: without a
# `trusted_proxies` option it replaces the header with the address it saw.
# Keep it that way — adding `trusted_proxies` here lets any caller choose the
# address the api sees.
#
# Check the syntax without installing Caddy (from the repository root):
#   docker run --rm -v "$PWD/infra/pilot/Caddyfile.example:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:ro" \
#     caddy:2-alpine caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
# On the host: copy this to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, edit the names, then
# `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` and `systemctl reload caddy`.
{
	# The address Let's Encrypt writes to about certificate expiry. Optional.
	# email ops@example.com
}

# The agent panel. REST goes to the api directly (one hop); everything else is
# the panel's static app, served by its own nginx.
panel.example.com {
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
	}
}

# The customer widget: loader.js, the iframe document and the hosted Chat page.
widget.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5174
}

# The api, called cross-origin by the widget and by customers' own integrations.
api.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
}

# RTM. Caddy passes the WebSocket upgrade through on its own.
rtm.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4001
}
